  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with zonal flow running from off \nthe coast of the Pacific Northwest through the PCNW and Montana. \nNorth of this is cooler air at the surface embedded in a broad \ntrough over the southwestern quadrant of Canada that is leaking \ndown into the Plains and northeast Montana. To the south a large \nclosed low sits over the Four Corners with a ridge just west of it\nrunning north from the Baja and cresting over northern \nCalifornia. \n\nToday: The zonal flow jet stream will drag pacific moisture in \nthe mid and high levels across the region generatng clouds today \nwhich could reach near broken to overcast status at high levels. \nThe ceilometers across the region can not measure above 12kft and\nare reporting SKC which is leading to a strong feedback effect \nwith no clouds in the current model data. Meanwhile, the ridge \nover Cali will begin expanding and widen into the Intermountain \nwest bumping up against the zonal flow to the north. \n\nTonight into Friday: The jet stream near the US/Canadian border \nwill go from a zonal position to more of a W-NW orientation as the\nridge to the south presses on it. A subtle shortwave at the mid \nlevels is expected to move through this flow and could kick off a \nfew stratiform showers Thursday and Friday. Temps will be quite \ncool Thursday with surface high pressure in place and should be \nthe coolest day in this forecast period. Thursday night into \nFriday morning could experience some frost on over valley bottoms \nif skies manage to clear out enough.\n\nSaturday into Monday: Surface high pressure over the Canadian \nprairies looks to be in place this weekend with flow becoming full\nNW. This will keep daytime temps slightly below normal and lead \nto dry conditions due to now very limited Pacific moisture. \n\nMonday night onward: Ensemble breakdown begins to occur as the \nnorthwest flow gets interrupted by the ridge to the southwest. \nThe placement and strength of this ridge axis looks to be in \nflux. Therefore, forecast confidence drops off here onward. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nDeviations to NBM include... Sky grids for the first 18 hours\nwhich used a 50/50 blend of NBM/CONSMOS. Effectively ceilometers\nare registering nothing below 12kft but Satellite is showing near\nBKN to OVC skies in some locations well above this height. The\nceilometers are having a feedback effect of removing clouds on\nmodel data which is then producing outrageously low or incorrect Sky\nvalues in the NBM ensemble output. \n\nGAH",
